# std.marker: the markers that say what may cross into another execution
# domain (spec/lang/10-modules.md#markers). The compiler answers `Send`,
# `Sync` and `Wire` from a type's parts, as it answers `Copy`; only std
# implements them.

## A trait with no members that says what may cross into one kind of domain.
## A trait that names it as a supertrait is a marker.
## See [module.marker.definition](../../spec/lang/10-modules.md#r-module.marker.definition).
pub trait Marker < ?Copy

## A value that may be copied, moved or lent to another thread of the same memory.
## See [module.marker.send](../../spec/lang/10-modules.md#r-module.marker.send).
pub trait Send < Marker

## A value that threads may read at once: it holds no counted buffer, so
## reading it changes no count.
## See [module.marker.sync](../../spec/lang/10-modules.md#r-module.marker.sync).
pub trait Sync < Marker

## A value that crosses into separate memory as a self-contained tree.
## See [module.marker.wire](../../spec/lang/10-modules.md#r-module.marker.wire).
pub trait Wire < Marker
